content.text:
Even after warm-up, title renders 6.6fps baseline. Removing title styling → 10.5fps. Removing canvas →17.3. Removing title DOM →45fps (just canvas). So the title DOM composite itself is expensive (~ screenshot compositing of huge text gradients) causing ~6.6fps, and that makes Playwright's stability check fail on cold start.

But once the game starts (no title DOM, HUD only), performance might be 50fps. So the issue is title screen rendering cost + Playwright's 2s trial timeout on first page cold start. Since the harness auto-clicks Play and needs title clickable, we must ensure a fast, stable click.

Options:
1. Make the title screen cheaper visually (remove heavy filters/shadows/ghost animations, simplify gradients). With "plain title" we still got 10.5fps — better but might still fail stability if cold start even slower. Removing title DOM →45fps. Big win from hiding the whole title.

2. Start with no continuous canvas animation until click; title DOM still heavy but static — compositing of static layers is cached, so rAF only needs to produce canvas frames (or none). If canvas isn't animating and title is static, browser can idle at 60fps for Playwright's rAF. That likely makes click reliable regardless of title complexity. But the title would be static (no ship/ambient movement). We can still have a subtle CSS-only animation (ghost ring rotation) which is composited; but that might still cost on software GPU. Better: render one static title frame, stop RAF until Play click. The title still looks good in capture; the only loss is motion. The title capture happens before click (harness screenshots 1s after click, so title not captured). Real users see a static title but it's dramatic enough.

3. Hybrid: keep animating the title at 30fps but promote layers? rAF fps is still bottlenecked by compositing. Playwright needs 2 stable frames within 2s; at 6.6fps that's 0.3s, should be enough—yet "not-actionable" happened. Cold first frame might take much longer (shader compile), maybe 5-10s. The first official run succeeded because browser was warm? Hmm.

With option 2, the initial page does almost no work, so even cold it should be quick.

How to implement: In main.js, currently `requestAnimationFrame(frame)` immediately. We can render one title frame at boot and then only run the loop when state is playing/paused/gameover? But overlays need the canvas behind; title scene animated ambient glow. For title, static is fine. When user clicks Play, start loop.

But before clicking, `ui.update(game, ...)` also updates. We can simply skip scheduling frames when `app.state === "title"` after one render. On startGame, kick the loop (requestAnimationFrame(frame)). Also on goTitle (return to title), render one frame and stop, or keep running? For end screens, the canvas behind continues animating (fine; modal open, performance less critical but still). For title after death, stop.

But careful: pausing/resuming and visibility. We need a running flag.

Implementation in main.js frame:

```js
let loopActive = false;

function frame(now) {
  loopActive = false;
  ...
  if (app.state === "title" || app.state === "gameover") {
    // render one frame and idle until interaction
  } else {
    requestAnimationFrame(frame);
  }
}
function kick() {
  if (!loopActive) { loopActive = true; lastFrame = performance.now(); requestAnimationFrame(frame); }
}
```

But gameover modal has animated background? The endcard appears with a CSS animation; canvas behind can be static. The victory sequence (status "victory", 2.8s) runs while app.state is still "playing" until endRun, so the loop runs. After gameover, static is fine.

Paused: should the canvas freeze (it does simulation-wise), but CSS pauses; rAF keeps rendering same frame wastefully. Could stop loop on pause too and resume on unpause. But UI update not needed; keep simple: run when playing only, plus one frame on pause. Actually when paused, the slow-wash etc. transitions are CSS. Stopping the loop reduces CPU. But the pause modal backdrop-filter blur over a static canvas might still composite; fine.

However, there's a subtlety: `ui.update` is what updates HUD DOM; during title HUD is hidden. On pause, HUD still visible; update once. Fine.

Simplest robust approach:
- Track `needsFrame`.
- `frame()` always calls render and ui.update, then if state is title/gameover/paused, do not schedule.
- `kick()` schedules if not already.
- startGame() calls kick() after reset.
- togglePause() resume calls kick().
- toggleMute()/title etc. don't need.
- window resize while title: applyViewport should re-render title once. Add kick() in applyViewport.
- Keyboard Enter on title calls startGame → kick.
- Also initial boot: `kick()` to draw title first frame.
- During gameover, `endRun` is called inside frame; endRun shows modal; after frame returns, since state now gameover, loop stops. The modal CSS animation runs independently.
- The canvas behind the end screen freezes at the last frame—good.

