Debt fuses are 9s game time. The test steals ~2.5s over 2.6s wall (the game slows time), then waits 7.8s wall. `debt:2` remained → not matured. Game time lag due to slow frames/screenshots.

Our changes to stop loop on title don't affect gameplay. The screenshot at line 98 may block. The whole test is CPU-intensive; frames maybe 20fps → with dt clamp 0.05, game advances 0.05*20=1s per wall second → 7.8s wall = 7.8s game? Actually at 20fps, dt=0.05, game advances exactly wall (20*0.05=1). If frames are slower than 20fps (e.g., 10fps), game advances half speed. The screenshots stall.

Now perf says 60fps in headless. But during test, screenshots and evaluate polling at 90ms intervals compete. Still, game time should roughly track wall time if fps >=20. The failures indicate ~30-50% slowdown, matching ~10-15fps.

Robust approach: substep simulation using wall-clock elapsed, capped at e.g. 0.25s per frame. That makes debt fuses mature in wall time even at 10fps (catch-up 6 substeps of 0.05). The risk: at very low fps, catch-up makes the game run fast-forward relative to visible frames. That's desirable for timers; gameplay may become harder because enemies move large steps, but substeps handle collision at 0.05 granularity.

